  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) Investigate laser-driven proton acceleration using petawatt femtosecond irradiation of structured, laser-tailored polymer foils: reduced-transverse-size foils enhance target normal sheath acceleration, and laser-tailored foils enhance Coulomb repulsion so energetic protons are accelerated by a hybrid mechanism of radiation pressure and Coulomb repulsion.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) High temporal contrast is a prerequisite for petawatt femtosecond laser experiments on structured targets, and petawatt-laser temporal contrast, pointing stability, and polymer-foil transverse shape were controlled in the experiment."];
  "Node_10" [label="(22,3,0) Coulomb repulsion is enhanced in laser-tailored foils by suppressing the conduction of cold electrons, which combined with radiation pressure forms a hybrid acceleration mechanism."];
  "Node_4" [label="(0,0,0) If employing conduction-restricted nanometre targets is an effective design to increase proton energy, then applying a multi-petawatt femtosecond laser to such targets will further enhance proton acceleration."];
  "R2" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-Conclusion: Enhanced proton acceleration in conduction-restricted nanometre targets is explained by laser-tailored foils enhancing Coulomb repulsion through suppressing cold-electron conduction."];
